Experiment on Wireshark to sniff encrypted packets.

Note: This experiment was done solely for personal educational purposes. I encourage anyone reading this document to try it on their own without following the steps below exactly, or to create a different experiment following the same philosophy. For the creation of the login.html and server_https.py files, I relied entirely on AI; this is not an experiment to learn programming.

1)Generating the RSA key and certificate

openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -keyout nuovakey_rsa.pem \
  -out questocert.pem -days 365 -nodes
nuovakey_rsa.pem: RSA private key (no passphrase, for Wireshark). Important that the key is RSA.

questocert.pem: self-signed certificate containing the public key.
A self-signed certificate is sufficient for a LAN experiment, and the RSA key is used both by the server to encrypt and by Wireshark to decrypt.

2)Protocol and cipher details to understand what we’re doing
We avoid TLS 1.3 with PFS because Wireshark cannot decrypt it with only the RSA key—it would require capturing the session secrets too. PFS (Perfect Forward Secrecy) is a property that changes keys for each session using mechanisms like Diffie-Hellman (DH) or Elliptic Curve DH (ECDHE).

2.1) Explaining Diffie-Hellman, Elliptic Curve DH (ECDHE), and the AES128-SHA cipher suite

Diffie-Hellman (DH)
Purpose: lets two parties generate a shared secret key over a public channel, without ever transmitting the key itself.

How it works in practice:

Both agree on public numbers:

A large prime p

A base g

Alice picks secret a and computes A = gᵃ mod p.

Bob picks secret b and computes B = gᵇ mod p.

Alice sends A to Bob; Bob sends B to Alice.

Alice computes (B)ᵃ mod p = gᵃᵇ mod p.
Bob computes (A)ᵇ mod p = gᵃᵇ mod p.
Both now share the secret gᵃᵇ mod p, without ever sending it directly.

Classical DH often uses static keys, which do not guarantee PFS if reused—that’s why we have ECDHE.

Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman (ECDHE)

Elliptic-curve version of DH, using points on a curve instead of modular exponentiation.

Faster and more secure at the same key length.

“E” stands for Ephemeral: keys are generated per session ⇒ PFS enabled (which we deliberately avoid in this experiment).

How it works (high-level):

Server and client exchange elliptic-curve public keys.

Each uses its private key to compute a common point on the curve.

From that point, they derive a shared secret.

In TLS, ECDHE is used (with RSA or ECC certificates) for authentication; key exchange itself is ephemeral ⇒ PFS.
Because of PFS, even with the server’s RSA private key you cannot decrypt recorded TLS 1.3 traffic—you only negotiate ephemeral session keys.

AES128-SHA

AES-128: symmetric cipher used to encrypt the actual data.

SHA: hash function for integrity (HMAC).
It’s an RSA-based key-exchange suite without PFS, so Wireshark can decrypt using only the private key.

3) Forcing ALPN to use HTTP/1.1
ALPN (Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation) negotiates which protocol runs over TLS (e.g. http/1.1 vs. h2). Chrome defaults to HTTP/2 (where Wireshark doesn’t show http.request.method). Forcing HTTP/1.1 returns classic HTTP/1.1 frames.

4)Start and test

cd esperimentoW
python3 server_https.py
# Server listening on https://0.0.0.0:8443
From another device on the same LAN, visit https://<server-IP>:8443
Accept the self-signed certificate.
Fill in the form with user123 / password456 (the server checks exactly these credentials).

Capture traffic with Wireshark

Capture filter: tcp port 8443 (optionally add ip.addr and ip.src)

Import RSA key in TLS preferences:

IP: 0.0.0.0 (or your server IP)

Port: 8443

Protocol: http (not https)

Key File: path to nuovakey_rsa.pem

Capture from the start: ClientHello, ServerHello, etc.
Only then can Wireshark associate the RSA key with the session.

View decrypted POST
Once handshake and login complete, apply display filter:

http.request.method == "POST"
Wireshark will show in cleartext:

POST /login HTTP/1.1
Host: <IP>:8443
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

username=user123&password=password456
